# CONTROL Lua — Agent Instructions

Rules for coding agents that write CONTROL (AoE2Control) Lua modules. Look up every function, signature and enum in `CONTROL_LUA_ENGINE_REFERENCE.md`. If that file lacks a detail, check the [AoE2Control Documentation](https://aoe2control.github.io/).

## API

1. Use only functions, methods, tables and enums from the reference. CONTROL is not the AoE2 `.per` AI language. There is no `GetLocalPlayer()`; use `GetAssignedPlayer()` and `GetAssignedPlayerId()`.
2. Never guess enum names or numbers, and never hard-code ids for units, technologies, facts or ages. `Age` (in-match) and `OptionsAge` (match setup) have different values.
3. Call methods on objects with `:` (`player:GetTownCenters()`) and table functions with `.` (`Settings.AddBool(...)`, `IPC.Send(...)`).
4. Create strategic components with `ResourceTracker.new()`, `VillagerOccupation.new(tracker)`, `ConstructionPlacement.new(occupation)` in `Init()`. `ResourceTracker()` does not work.
5. `Color(r, g, b, a)` with integers takes `0` to `255`; with any float argument it takes `0` to `1`. `Color(1, 1, 1)` is almost black.

## Callbacks

6. Define callbacks as globals: `function Update()`, not `local function Update()`. CONTROL calls `Load(playerId)`, `Init()`, `Update()`, `Render()`, `End(hasWon)` and `Unload()`; all are optional.
7. Call `Settings.Add*` in `Load()`. Do not read game state in `Load()`; it can run in the main menu.
8. Give game commands (`TrainUnit`, `UnitsMove`, `ResearchTechnology` and the rest) only in `Update()`. With **Sequential Actions** on (the default), only the first command per `Update()` runs.
9. Call `Render*` draw functions only inside `Render()`. Anywhere else they raise an error. Keep expensive work out of `Render()`; it runs every frame.
10. A callback, including the file's top-level code, is stopped with an error after 1 second. Never write loops that wait for game state; `IPC.WaitForMessage` waits at most 500 ms.
11. After an error in `Update()` or `Render()`, CONTROL stops calling it until the module is reloaded. Guard code paths that can fail.

## Nil checks and stale objects

12. Check for `nil` before use: `GetAssignedPlayer()`, `GetPlayerById()`, `GetObjectById()`, `GetMapTile()`, `GetCurrentGameOptions()`, `Object:GetTargetObject()`, `Object:GetCurrentMapTile()`, `Player:GetSelectedObject()`, `GetFact()` and `ParseJSON()` can return `nil`.
13. Keep object ids or positions across callbacks, not `Object` values. A kept object that went into the fog or no longer exists raises an error on most methods. Check `IsVisible()` or fetch it again.
14. Object lists do not include unfinished buildings; use `Player:GetFoundations()`. `Object:GetName()` is the current graphic's name and changes with the activity; use `GetTypeName()` or `GetUnitObjectType()` to identify a type.

## Modes

15. **Multithreading** or the **Agent Bridge** on: modules read a copy of the game state. `Render()` is not called with Multithreading. `GetFact`, `Object:GetAttribute`, `Object:GetObjectData`, `GetObjectTypeData`, `GetObjectTypeAttribute`, `CheckPlacement`, `CanPlaceObject` and `MapTile:IsBuildable` can return `nil`. Commands are queued, so `true` means queued, not done. Strategic components raise an error.
16. **Tournament Mode**: commands outside `Update()` are refused, draw calls draw nothing, and `Log`, `SendChatMessage`, `SetCameraPosition`, the `Dispatch*` and random-map functions, replay and speed functions, render queries and most `GameOptions` methods do nothing. Do not rely on `Log()` output there.
17. Modules run in multiplayer only when the match has cheats enabled.

## Sandbox and files

18. `io`, `os`, `debug`, `coroutine`, `utf8` and `package` do not exist. `load`, `loadfile`, `dofile`, `module` and `collectgarbage` are removed. Use `Log()`, not `print()`. Talk to other programs through `IPC`.
19. Name the entry file `{moduleName}.main.lua` (letters, digits, underscores) inside its own folder under `modules\`. Keep it short and load other files with `require("folder.file")`: up to four dot-separated parts, relative to the entry file's folder, no `..`.
